I’m using the Angular testing guidance now because this failure sits at the form/hydration boundary; it will guide the smallest durable wait and its verification.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4227","summary":["**Investigating form field value resetting**"]},{"type":"contextCompaction","id":"item-4228"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4229","text":"The PR is merged. I’m finishing the required post-merge path: the main-branch E2E gate caught a second CI-only profile-form hydration race, so staging correctly stayed blocked. I’m turning that into a small follow-up fix, rerunning the complete local gate, then I’ll merge it and verify the deployment chain.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4230","text":"The failure artifact confirms there was no save/RPC failure—the dynamically opened Signal Form reset the PayPal input before submission. I’m fixing the test at the actual readiness boundary: wait for the dialog form to finish hydration and expose all seeded values before editing any field. This avoids masking the race with retries.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4231","summary":["**Identifying email input sanitization issue**","**Testing input behavior and form updates**","**Identifying email input sanitization issue**","**Testing input behavior and form updates**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4232","text":"I’ve reproduced this under a ten-run stress setup on the exact merged SHA. The local stack is deliberately running with one worker to match CI timing; once the baseline observation completes, I’ll apply the hydration/readiness assertion and run the same stress test again.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4233","summary":["**Planning reuse of authenticated state**","**Planning form input value assertions**","**Planning reuse of authenticated state**","**Planning form input value assertions**","**Planning reuse of authenticated state**","**Planning form input value assertions**","**Designing robust input typing helper**","**Analyzing form control value resets and test timing issues**","**Investigating PayPal input blank issue and test stability**","**Analyzing form control value resets and test timing issues**","**Investigating PayPal input blank issue and test stability**","**Diagnosing PayPal field reset due to missing blur events**","**Analyzing form control value resets and test timing issues**","**Investigating PayPal input blank issue and test stability**","**Diagnosing PayPal field reset due to missing blur events**"]},{"type":"fileChange","id":"exec-53e65a1b-d511-4f40-a8a0-53becc5f10b5","status":"completed","changes":[{"path":"/Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/b7e9/evorto/.changeset/stabilize-profile-edit-e2e.md","kind":{"type":"add"}},{"path":"/Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/b7e9/evorto/tests/specs/profile/user-profile-edit.spec.ts","kind":{"type":"update","move_path":null}}]},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4234","summary":["**Testing E2E with persistent Docker stack**","**Planning sequential test invocations to ensure unique seeds**","**Planning sequential test invocations to ensure unique seeds**","**Evaluating repeat-each versus separate runs for test reliability**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4235","text":"The fix’s first execution passed; the stress command then exposed an unrelated fixture limitation: `--repeat-each` reuses the same deterministic tenant ID, so repetitions 2–10 collide on the database primary key. I’m switching to the repository’s supported full-suite run, which gives each collected test its normal isolated fixture and is the result that matters for merge eligibility.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4236","summary":["**Planning full local test suite rerun**","**Planning Docker cleanup before e2e**","**Outlining local test and build sequence**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4237","text":"The focused repaired scenario is green on a freshly reset stack. I’m now running the complete pre-push gate—units, PostgreSQL integration, builds, Terraform/image checks, all Playwright projects, the 11-test provider suite, and live ESNcard certification. No CI-triggering push will happen unless the collected local suite is entirely green.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4238","text":"Core checks are clean so far: 1,406 server tests, 799 app tests, and all 55 PostgreSQL integration tests passed with no incomplete outcomes. Next are the production build plus Terraform/static infrastructure checks, then the Linux/amd64 image security and size gate.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4239","text":"The production build and Terraform gate are green, including zero Terraform security misconfigurations. The amd64 runtime image gate is still building/scanning; after that I’ll reset the disposable stack and run every Playwright project from scratch so the earlier focused-test fixture residue cannot affect results.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4240","summary":["**Investigating Auth0 callback URL mismatch**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4241","text":"The application tests themselves have not failed. The clean baseline is blocked before them by Auth0: the first login never renders a username field, then fail-fast correctly marks all remaining tests incomplete. I’m checking the generated worktree port against the callback URLs now; this matches the local-port constraint we discussed earlier, not the profile fix.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4242","summary":["**Investigating Bun .env auto-loading behavior**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4243","text":"Confirmed: this worktree generated port `4355`, while the Auth0 dev tenant is configured for the default `4200` callback. Port 4200 is free. I’m rerunning the clean suite with `APP_HOST_PORT=4200`, exactly matching your earlier decision; no tracked configuration change is needed.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4244","text":"Auth0 is now past login on port 4200 and the 150-test baseline is actively executing. The repaired profile scenario is part of this run; there are no failures or incomplete markers so far.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"userMessage","id":"item-4245","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do you think about [vercel-labs/portless](https://github.com/vercel-labs/portless) for our general issues with auth0
"}]},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4246","summary":["**Clarifying tool concurrency constraints**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4247","text":"Portless is directionally a very good fit here because it replaces worktree-specific ports with stable named local origins—the exact thing Auth0 needs for allowlisted callbacks. I’m checking its current routing/TLS/CLI behavior and Auth0 constraints before recommending whether to adopt it repo-wide; the docs suite continues running meanwhile.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"webSearch","id":"exec-d261a93a-6ff2-4ee6-87f2-e84981da2a66","query":"https://github.com/vercel-labs/portless","action":{"type":"openPage","url":"https://github.com/vercel-labs/portless"}},{"type":"webSearch","id":"exec-80f65389-3d41-40ea-bcbf-3c2c8d27132a","query":"site:github.com/vercel-labs/portless README portless local development wildcard domains Auth0","action":{"type":"search","query":"site:github.com/vercel-labs/portless README portless local development wildcard domains Auth0","queries":null}},{"type":"webSearch","id":"exec-57dd832f-1ebf-4e76-a069-b67e42421df1","query":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vercel-labs/portless/main/README.md","action":{"type":"openPage","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vercel-labs/portless/main/README.md"}},{"type":"webSearch","id":"exec-f250629c-a3fd-4163-8482-97ccb0a3db93","query":"site:auth0.com/docs allowed callback URLs wildcard subdomain localhost port Auth0 ...","action":{"type":"search","query":null,"queries":["site:auth0.com/docs allowed callback URLs wildcard subdomain localhost port Auth0","site:auth0.com/docs secure local development callback URL HTTPS localhost custom domain"]}},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4248","summary":["**Assessing Auth0 wildcard subdomain support**","**Preparing interim assessment commentary**","**Clarifying Portless usage and configuration details**","**Preparing interim assessment commentary**","**Clarifying Portless usage and configuration details**","**Adjusting local detection for localhost domains**","**Verifying auth callback and cookie domain handling**","**Adjusting local detection for localhost domains**","**Verifying auth callback and cookie domain handling**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4249","text":"My take: yes—Portless is probably the cleanest long-term local solution, but I would adopt it in a separate follow-up rather than expand this merge repair.

Why it fits:

- It gives the main checkout `https://evorto.localhost` and each worktree a stable branch subdomain such as `https://fix-ui.evorto.localhost`, while assigning internal ports automatically. That removes our need to register every generated port. [Portless README](https://github.com/vercel-labs/portless)
- In the dev-only Auth0 application, we could register the exact main callback plus one wildcard worktree callback: `https://evorto.localhost/callback` and `https://*.evorto.localhost/callback`. Auth0 supports a wildcard in the left-most subdomain for first-party applications, one level only; it explicitly discourages this for production, which is fine for our isolated dev tenant. [Auth0 subdomain placeholders](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/applications/wildcards-for-subdomains)
- Default HTTPS also gives us behavior closer to staging and correct Secure/SameSite-cookie behavior. [Auth0 local HTTPS guidance](https://auth0.com/docs/libraries/secure-local-development)

It is not quite plug-and-play for this repo. We would need a small integration slice:

- Teach the runtime generator to accept Portless’s injected `PORT` and `PORTLESS_URL`.
- Treat `*.localhost` as loopback for our tenant-cookie fallback; currently only bare `localhost` qualifies.
- Verify the proxy’s real `Host` and forwarded-proto behavior against our hardened request boundary.
- Keep CI on its current exact `localhost:4200` origin; Portless is pre-1.0 and should not become a CI dependency.
- Pin a tested Portless version and add source tests for the URL/runtime contract.

So: strong recommendation for local dev/worktrees, with the Auth0 wildcard restricted to the dev tenant. It should eliminate this entire class of port bookkeeping without weakening production callback rules.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4250","text":"All Playwright suites required for the ordinary gate are now clean: 150/150 functional, 52/52 docs, and the full protected-provider suite 11/11—zero skips, retries, or flakes. The last local release gate is live ESNcard certification, including its provider-error unit coverage.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"contextCompaction","id":"item-4251"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4252","text":"Portless looks promising enough for a separate local-development improvement, and the current repair is now fully green locally. I’m committing that repair, publishing it for review, then I’ll carry it through merge and verify the post-merge staging path.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4253","text":"The repair is committed cleanly on top of the current remote `main`. I’m doing the repository’s final stack synchronization now, then I’ll open the focused PR with the complete local evidence attached.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4254","text":"PR [#99](https://github.com/evorto-app/app/pull/99) is open and mergeable; all required jobs are queued. Its scope is test-only, and the PR description records the complete zero-skip local gate. I’ll merge it as soon as GitHub’s required review and checks clear.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4255","text":"The review is clean so far: CodeRabbit, Terraform, release metadata, stack validation, and PostgreSQL integration have passed. The application build/unit job and Linux image security gate are the only remaining checks.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4256","text":"All PR checks are green, including `CI/gate`, the image/SBOM/vulnerability gate, and CodeRabbit. I’m 