the verifier’s final size/SBOM/vulnerability summary. I’m treating that as an incomplete gate, checking whether a lingering BuildKit process or the generated Terraform cache inflated the context, and will rerun it to a real terminal result.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4610","summary":["**Identifying Docker build context bloat sources**","**Planning .dockerignore update to exclude Terraform**","**Identifying Docker build context bloat sources**","**Planning .dockerignore update to exclude Terraform**","**Planning .dockerignore security enhancements**","**Adding dockerignore pattern tests**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4611","text":"The duplicate image runs exposed a separate deployment-relevant defect: `.dockerignore` did not exclude Terraform’s local providers/state/tfvars, so staging’s post-`terraform init` build could send hundreds of megabytes—and potentially developer-only Terraform configuration—to BuildKit. That also explains the earlier oversized/hanging build behavior. I’m adding a regression-guarded context exclusion before rerunning the gate once, cleanly and without duplicate scanners.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"fileChange","id":"exec-de55792b-69b1-4d64-b2be-1e8ba8fe52e3","status":"completed","changes":[{"path":"/Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/b7e9/evorto/.changeset/verify-scaleway-database-server-identity.md","kind":{"type":"update","move_path":null}},{"path":"/Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/b7e9/evorto/.dockerignore","kind":{"type":"update","move_path":null}},{"path":"/Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/b7e9/evorto/helpers/testing/container-image-pinning-source.spec.ts","kind":{"type":"update","move_path":null}}]},{"type":"contextCompaction","id":"item-4612"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4613","text":"Portless looks like a strong dev-only solution here: stable HTTPS hostnames can replace Auth0’s unbounded localhost ports with one wildcard callback, while staging and production retain exact URLs. I’m keeping the default-port setup as the fallback and finishing the Scaleway deployment fix before proposing a trial.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4614","text":"The new regression test passes. I also found and fixed a deployment-specific issue that likely contributed to the stalled image build: Terraform’s local cache and state artifacts were entering the Docker build context, inflating it from a few megabytes to roughly 680 MB. Those files and all dotenv variants are now excluded.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4615","summary":["**Planning sequential logging with tee**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4616","text":"The amended commit is clean (`177f0a75b1a`). I’m now rerunning the complete local release gate from that exact commit; I won’t push or open the PR until every collected test—including protected provider coverage—passes with zero incomplete outcomes.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4617","text":"Preflight and all 1,417 server/unit-helper tests are green. I’m continuing through the Angular unit suite, PostgreSQL integration, build, Terraform/security/image checks, and then all Playwright/provider projects.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4618","text":"Angular unit tests (799), PostgreSQL integration tests (55), and the production build are green. The runtime image hardening is next; this run will also verify that Terraform working data no longer enters the Docker context.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4619","text":"Terraform validation and static infrastructure scanning are clean with zero findings. The single image-security run is still active; I’m polling that same build rather than restarting it, so we avoid the Docker-image race encountered earlier.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4620","text":"The image gate is green: Docker context is down to 640 KB, the verified uncompressed runtime is 152,124,275 bytes, and the vulnerability scan reports zero findings. I’m moving into the browser suites now on the registered default Auth0 port.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4621","text":"The browser runner itself has not collected tests yet: it correctly refused to take disposable ownership of an already-running PostgreSQL stack. I’m resuming that initialized stack explicitly, then I’ll rerun the full baseline unchanged.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4622","text":"The initialized stack resume is taking longer than expected and has not produced an error. I’m inspecting its exact container state now rather than resetting local data; if the existing one-shot initialization is incomplete, the resume guard will identify that explicitly.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"reasoning","id":"item-4623","summary":["**Planning container restart for Playwright**"]},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4624","text":"The retained stack was healthy on port 4200, but `.env.dev` had regenerated 4355, which explains Playwright’s failed readiness probe. I’ve stopped the stuck resume request and am rebuilding a fresh, exact-checkout stack explicitly on 4200—the Auth0-registered default—before rerunning the browser gate.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4625","text":"The exact-checkout images are built, but Docker Desktop is again stalling during the container-start API call even though it previously reported the services as running. I’m checking health and daemon responsiveness directly; this is local Docker orchestration, not an application-test failure.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMessage","id":"item-4626","text":"Docker’s daemon responds to reads and builds but every container-start request times out; only this Evorto project exists, and all its containers are still in `Created`. I’m restarting Docker Desktop now, then I’ll reuse the already-built exact-checkout images and continue the gate.","phase":"commentary"},{"type":"agentMes